@Matti_Vuori This pattern of credit bothers me. I would not be certain that Torvalds was alone creating this, and I can easily see that there's A LOT of contributors since. Anyone could change it since software is malleable. And I am certain many have. https://github.com/git/git/graphs/contributors

Git developers are plural. 

@queenofnewyork

@datengaertner That was not written for test automation (and writing for it, I would consider an entirely different format). It was also not sourced from any legal obligations to do so. 

If I had not talked to the tester who wrote this, I would not have any ideas that there was a risk on their mind related to recent changes. 

Also, you can write this all with just a few lines, without magic variables and explaining what is design and what is accidental.

@gem_hill On my first ever book, I found a publisher first. I made a contract and built an imagined wall of quality where I was unable to deliver. The only reminder of that book is that you can still find its cover from bookstores by searching online.

I needed to bring down that quality wall and self-publish. The people willing to read chapter by chapter was rewarding and helped me go on. 

In end of having self-published, I have had offers of moving the book to publisher but I chose not to.

@autiomaa Why do you think I ended up doing 550 talks so far - I did the practice and got stuck in a loop. :D 

I used to pass out having to go in front of an audience to introduce myself. It took a lot of determination and work to get away from it. 

There is a reason why I run a speaker mentorship program and have helped a lot of people on this. Just today mentored yet another great person. 

@steve

Next up a disability talk (not accessibility talk) as Magali Milbergue frames her talk ‘The Invisible Elephant in the Room’. #newcrafts

20% of population have disabilities, 80% of those invisible. You might not know and see the challenges your colleagues at work navigate.

@daisymoon

@EsterDaniel Depends on who you ask. There is a product owner who decides priorities but people within the dev team make a lot of those decisions. 

Kind of like this: 

"If it’s your decision to make, it’s design. If it’s not, it’s a requirement." -Alistair Cockburn

@rvr We had this conversation how comb shapes change over time, not just by growing a skills, but also by just not having always the time to attend to something you'd be skilled in if you had time. 

Paint drop sounds to be possibly nicer way of framing this. Will google for his writing.

And now I noted the link - paint drip feels more same shape than broken comb.

@sfy We usually do, any of the tens of contractors. But choosing one from contractor lines is just as hard as finding right one to employ. 

Why would your seniors be better at this than the other seniors? Why should they be paid to learn over the juniors I am considering?

Half of our ranks are contractors.

@tokyo_0 @kushal I find that there is a difference between what an individual must personally be able to do, and what we must be capable of collaborating with in the team. 

Organizations of software where there is no software except as users / customers presenting as organizations of "software professionals" is my pet peeve. 

Individuals can have a variety of abilities in programming, but avoiding it all together is weird.

@fishbowler why tell one story if you can tell three? Tell them all, and you might find an alternative story gives you insight you didn’t have. 

Also, if you choose to tell one, if possible make it one that serves good. I’ve occasionally found the three questions of true, kind and necessary help me with not sharing every story. 

There’s always a perspective. Never an absolute ‘truth’. Software is full of stories of growth.

@tero My Powerpoint-fu gives me that much, after all I used to test that back in the days. :D 

But today I learned that one of the fonts I use may have been removed due to problematic creator and the font I licensed gives a "Font not available" error. 

Good thing is that this is my last talk :)

@Stoori I’ve used mastopoet mostly on getting a thing permalinked in a blog post. The amount of meaningless blog content I have from twitter-times after deleting my account still hurts. 

I did not want to impose UBI on top of a great standalone the original had. Especially since I think UBI is not a replacement for companies paying for the artists and I still expect someone to jump to explain that to me. 

This is my first ever public mention of UBI.

@nemeciii We have had automated test run on all merges, and nowadays there's a decent amount / coverage in them. Continuous exploratory testing in production-like environment feeds into growing automation. 

I would absolutely not go back to one deploy per sprint or one deploy per night. We're 3 teams, 20 people. 

In about a year, I can move "QA" as you call it to happen while in production.

@lukadotnet I am trying to get designers to do css tweaking with devs (pairing) and I would really need single track for that to even be feasible. But we have very siloed roles deeply ingrained. I wonder if I am fighting a lost battle on this one.

Then again, the product perspective of making decisions on "this or that" sometimes needs so much research with users and customers that we can't stick with single track. 

Dual track rarely works.

@weltraumpirat the fundamental challenge continues to deliver unmalleable code also in the future. And I need to fix the future as well as the bug. 

At least concrete examples make good conversations and some conversations change future actions. 

But you know all this. Not the first rodeo for either of us and all that. :)

@iwein It does not deserve a boost, but probably deserves a clarification. 

I have a basic idea of "nothing changes if you change nothing", as in ideas turn to code only by changing the code. So I was trying out if I could (or should) calculate the ratio of people who contribute in code to those who contribute to the ideas of those who contribute to code. 

Kind of like dependency ratio of a nation, over 100% means you have more dependants than tax payers.

@tastapod hush, my contracts say explicitly I’m not allowed to. At least both of us are allowed to say things on the internets, I’m sure that is about to be limited one day too. 

I suggested he could read my blog. But I’d really just prefer to talk to lovely people who assume I know stuff and trust that I will ask when I don’t because that is possible. 

I have a teenage boy at home who also explains things to me. There is no power in this world to correct this pattern of ‘conversation’.

@Luketoop 
I see people not stepping up to the most basic level of self-organising, considering everything as external. It is never that they did not ask, it it that they were not told. It is not that they did not search for information, it was not spooned to them. Hiding into status and power is assigning power you hold to others. 
@dahukanna @junosz @cornazano @ez

