BBST-kurssisarja on loistava ja siinä opetellaan testaten avoimen lähdekoodin sovelluksia. 

Testauksesta on sertifiointi joka ei opeta testausta vaan sanastoa. Moni kokee sen oleelliseksi.

@gdinwiddie Back in my days of consulting, I was asked to assess a test suite a company was very proud of. It had been created a few years earlier by contractor as hit and run gig, and the company had been maintaining it since. 

Turns out maintaining meant removing all assertions. Removing SUT would have failed but nothing more did. 

But it was success - they trusted the useless piece enough to not do same manually, and changed nothing that would have needed to be tested. Dumb luck.

@economics The requirements failure is believing there are requirements not a problem we are seeking to solve.

I'm curious on this Standford's game. Would you happen to know where I could read more about it?

I found one about contractor chains from MIT from wikipedia, might be more too? https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_distribution_game

@faassen So does rockstar programming language. :D 
https://codewithrockstar.com

I wonder if they would find me some if I asked for rockstar programmers.

@testerab I unlinked a bunch of them, still thinking if they’d need a world where they don’t see me to correct me. I know I need a purge of the sadness they inflict on me. 

It’s not one but this clique that saddens me. The one who shows up to say how he is entitled to his view. The same one who called me to scold me on ‘not being supportive to new enthusiastic voices’ when I told another of them to not plagiarize. 

Weird how this is…

@jawnsy I just wanted to point out that even I can’t remember her name, even if the guy on stage tried introducing her. It’s so built in on how we remember and assign credit. 

I notice when I do that and because I notice I feel extra discouraged. 

In the end we’re all part of the masses and I’d love better sampling.

@osma Mä olen yrittänyt selittää sitä sillä että ajan investointi tuottaa osaamista sekä syvempiä tuloksia, ja huonolaatuinenkin testaus on testausta. Siksi meidän pitäisi katsoa tuloksia eikä organisoitumista. 

On paljon erikoistuneita testaajia, jotka eivät pääse tekemään mitään testailua syvemmälle koska testaajan olemassaolo luo ajatuksen että testaus kuuluu testaajalle.

Testailu on kyllä hieno sana. Hitsit.

@pelavarre Enough words wouldn't reach anyone. Walking away from a conversation is an option. 

Feels like you wanted to connect, and share a joke. Feels like that is inspired by a mix of things you think and connected with what I wrote. 

Is there a connection I can help with? I enjoy meeting people over mastodon.

@fishbowler I sense frustration and confusion in daily meetings, and from lack of progress. It is a little slower to realize but offering help still happens. 

This conversation comes from *juniors* to managers, and being new during this time, they have no comparison point. 

A lot of senior folks I see jump as much (if not more, but invisibly) to help out a colleague.

@mathiasverraes Not every great writer is a great speaker. Audience pays good money to attend and travel to conferences, and they deserve to see great presentations. 

I hate the whole "competition" framing (as you may already know). There are experiences that would change things that never make it to stages with competition mindset. I experimented a lot with that with ETC. 

When we care for audiences, we can curate contents in new ways. But it is a time investment to LISTEN to people as audit.

@tottinge Hmm, you surprised me with the framing of 'shame'. Very insightful. 

Whenever I think of where I learned about working with shame, I think of Brene Brown. 

I may do something to add to sense of shame, but it is also often something that already sits with us. I was thinking of this more as *show sooner* over *pingpong of information*.

@joeposaurus So now I know that I could use I Ching, or Tarot or just dices, and they are valid approaches, the the first qualifies as truly weird. 

The idea that ancient Chinese gets translated to German that gets translated to English, that gets talked about for 1 hour 29 minutes has so many chaotic elements that this must be true.

Where do you find this stuff? 🤣

@mahryekuh It makes sense regularly, yes. And I already did it to the point of coming up with a schedule and sharing my email. Yet they don't pick up the next step other than saying their email. 

I feel like I am not up to recruiting yet another person who won't think of the overall experience but only the traditional distribution of responsibilities. 

Only reason I can afford to think about this is that there are so many people looking for positions right now.

@joshin4colours What promise do you see in it? 

I see it evolved to be at least debuggable. That makes it feel shiny if you have been around the earlier pain. But the whole design choices how RF language is "simplified" make it harder when working beyond hello world. 

To me, typescript and python have so much promise with their most common test frameworks. Promise of shared tools, not being the tester bottleneck. And better AI support.

@queenofnewyork Given time, focus and right assignment, finding some of the blind spots definitely happens. 

I have been constraining the time, asking for increased intent and a more varied idea of the blind spots. 

Because even a broken clock is right twice a day. Those with time and focus on testing need to do better. Many do. Many don't. Telling the difference is challenging enough that I still haven't managed to find a way to explain it well.

@iwein Talking to decision-maker sounds like a reasonable advice. 

I think the context is I am discovering my own incompetence and figuring out how to fix some of it. I am comfortable failing, but also curious on how I could be doing it better. 

This time I am failing at helping people realize transformation in practice. I can't be very specific about all the theories of reasons, even if a major one is that I try doing too many things at same time meaning not being available as necessary.

@poppis Full day of 5 people doing planning for software, with 5 people watching us, and 1 person watching the 5 people watching. 

Some of the low score is from the idea that 2 of the 5 watching were not supposed to be watching but part of the team and we misjudged that. AI impacting team dynamics was another one. 

Quite an experience of a job interview.
